= Glow Lamp =

A ring of 8 addressable LEDs driven by an ESP32-C3. WiFi is provisioned through
a captive portal, the lamp is controlled over a REST API and over MQTT, and the
firmware updates itself from GitHub releases.

Source: [https://github.com/brennanMKE/GlowLamp brennanMKE/GlowLamp]

== Hardware ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Part !! Detail
|-
| Board || ESP32-C3 (<code>esp32-c3-devkitm-1</code>)
|-
| Ring || WS2812B, 8 LEDs
|-
| Data pin || GPIO 4
|-
| Power || 5 V, capped in firmware at 500 mA
|}

Wire the ring's data line to GPIO 4 and share ground between the ring's supply
and the ESP32-C3. At the default brightness of 64 the 8 LEDs are well inside the
500 mA cap, so USB power is fine; run it near 255 and use a separate 5 V supply.

=== The ring is one light ===

This is the constraint the firmware is built around. Behind a diffuser, several
colors an inch apart do not read as several colors &mdash; they mix into a muddy
white. So the ring shows '''one color at a time''', and the effects differ in how
that color changes over time rather than in where it sits on the ring.

''Flicker'' is the deliberate exception: it relies on that mixing to look like a
flame, and is meant to be given colors that are already close together.

== Deployed lamps ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Name !! Hostname !! Location
|-
| Castor || <code>castor-lamp.local</code> ||
|-
| Pollux || <code>pollux-lamp.local</code> ||
|}

Each lamp has two names. The '''name''' is free text ("Castor"), shown in the UI
and announced over mDNS, and changes take effect immediately. The '''hostname'''
is the mDNS label, lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; the lamp answers at
<code><hostname>.local</code> and needs a reboot to republish it.

Give every lamp a distinct hostname. Two lamps sharing one would both answer to
it, and nothing would report the conflict.

== Finding a lamp ==

Lamps announce <code>_glowlamp._tcp</code> on port 80 over mDNS.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
./scripts/find_devices.sh                  # scan and report
./scripts/glowlamp.py discover             # the same thing
</syntaxhighlight>

mDNS does not cross VLANs or a guest network, so this only finds lamps on the
same subnet. An unprovisioned lamp is not on the network at all &mdash; look for
its setup access point instead.

== First-time setup ==

# Power on an unprovisioned lamp. It raises an access point named <code><hostname>-Setup-XXXXXX</code>.
# Join it; the captive portal opens.
# Pick the network, enter the password, save. The lamp reboots and joins.
# Find it with <code>./scripts/find_devices.sh</code> and open its page to set the name.

WiFi credentials are never compiled into the firmware, so a release binary is
safe to publish and any lamp can be re-provisioned in the field.

== Web interface ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Path !! What
|-
| <code>/</code> || Status, and the controls: on/off, brightness, identify
|-
| <code>/effects</code> || '''The everyday page''': effect, colors, presets, brightness, on/off
|-
| <code>/settings</code> || Firmware updates, names, MQTT broker, reboot
|-
| <code>/help</code> || The REST API, documented by the lamp itself
|-
| <code>/api</code> || The same reference as JSON
|-
| <code>/wifi</code> || WiFi setup pages
|}

'''Identify''' blinks the ring white for a few seconds at a floor brightness, so a
lamp dimmed to 5 in a bright room still announces itself. It overrides power and
restores whatever was showing, so identifying a lamp that is off leaves it off.

== Effects ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Effect !! What it does
|-
| <code>blend</code> || Holds on a color, then eases to the next. ''The default.''
|-
| <code>loop</code> || Walks the palette steadily, never resting on a color.
|-
| <code>flicker</code> || Similar colors mixing and guttering, like a flame. The one effect that lights the ring several colors at once.
|-
| <code>neon</code> || One color at full brightness, held steady, switching cleanly to the next.
|}

Set from <code>http://<lamp>/effects</code>, which carries one-tap presets (Flame,
Ocean, Sunset, Forest, Vivid) alongside the individual color pickers.

Up to '''5 colors'''. Colors are carried as hue and saturation rather than RGB, and
transitions travel the short way around the color wheel: an RGB blend between
two colors far apart on the wheel passes through gray and reads as dirty white
on a WS2812B.

An effect set over REST is '''temporary'''. It reverts to the default after 5
minutes unless told otherwise, is capped at 8 hours, and does not survive a
reboot &mdash; a lamp left mid-experiment finds its own way back. Anything set
over MQTT does '''not''' expire, because Home Assistant is a controller rather than
someone trying something out.

== REST API ==

Full reference: <code>docs/rest-api.md</code>, or <code>http://<lamp>/help</code> on the lamp itself.

{| class="wikitable"
! Method !! Path !! Body
|-
| GET || <code>/api/status</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/power</code> || <code>{"on": true &#124; false &#124; "toggle"}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/brightness</code> || <code>{"value": 0-255}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/identify</code> || <code>{"seconds": 1-60}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/effect</code> || <code>{"effect": "...", "colors": [...], "seconds": 300}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/effect/reset</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/name</code> || <code>{"name": "...", "hostname": "..."}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/broker</code> || <code>{"host": "...", "port": 1883}</code>
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/check</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/install</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/ota/update</code> ||
|-
| POST || <code>/api/reboot</code> ||
|}

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
curl http://castor-lamp.local/api/status
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/power?on=false'
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/brightness?value=128'
curl -X POST 'http://castor-lamp.local/api/effect?effect=neon&colors=ff0000,00ff00'
</syntaxhighlight>

'''A JSON body is only read when the request sets <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>.'''
Curl's default content type is form-encoded, and a JSON document sent that way
is discarded by the HTTP server before the firmware sees it &mdash; silently.
Query parameters never have that problem.

Anything that changes the lamp is POST only. There is no authentication: these
are LAN devices, and anything that can reach one can control it.

== MQTT ==

Full reference: <code>docs/mqtt.md</code>.

Set a broker and the lamp publishes a retained Home Assistant discovery config,
appearing as <code>light.<name></code> with brightness, a color picker and the effect
list &mdash; no YAML, no restart, no filesystem access to the Home Assistant host.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
./scripts/glowlamp.py broker 192.168.1.10 --host castor-lamp.local
</syntaxhighlight>

{| class="wikitable"
! Topic !! Direction !! Retained
|-
| <code>homeassistant/light/glowlamp_<mac>/config</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/state</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/availability</code> || publish || yes
|-
| <code>glowlamp/<hostname>/set</code> || subscribe || '''never'''
|}

Commands must never be retained: a retained command replays on every reconnect
and pins the lamp to whatever was last sent.

Command payloads use Home Assistant's JSON light schema. <code>state</code> must be the
'''word''' <code>"ON"</code> or <code>"OFF"</code> &mdash; a boolean is accepted silently and never
turns the lamp on.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
mosquitto_pub -h broker -t glowlamp/castor-lamp/set -m '{"state":"ON","brightness":200}'
mosquitto_pub -h broker -t glowlamp/castor-lamp/set -m '{"effect":"flicker"}'
</syntaxhighlight>

MQTT is optional and off by default. A lamp with no broker never connects and is
fully usable over REST, which stays the interface for the scripts, identify and
OTA.

== Home Assistant automations ==

Point a lamp at the broker and its entity appears on its own. Nothing is added to
<code>configuration.yaml</code>, nothing is restarted, and Home Assistant needs no
filesystem access &mdash; which is the whole reason the lamps speak MQTT. See
<code>docs/home-assistant-control.md</code> for why REST was rejected for this.

=== Entities ===

{| class="wikitable"
! Lamp !! Entity !! Supports
|-
| Castor || <code>light.castor</code> || on/off, brightness, color, effect
|-
| Pollux || <code>light.pollux</code> || on/off, brightness, color, effect
|}

These are ordinary light entities. They work in automations, scenes, dashboards
and voice like any other light, and they report state back &mdash; a lamp changed
from its own web page shows the new state in Home Assistant within a second.

'''Check the entity ID before writing anything against it.''' Home Assistant builds
it from the device name and the entity name, so a lamp that publishes both as
"Castor" becomes <code>light.castor_castor</code>, named "Castor Castor". Firmware that
publishes a null entity name gets the clean <code>light.castor</code>. Both lamps were
doubled on first connect under 0.0.5.

=== The schedule ===

The lamps follow the GlowKitchen strips, so the workshop lights behave as one.

{| class="wikitable"
! Automation !! Trigger !! Action
|-
| GlowLamp on at 9am || 09:00 || <code>light.turn_on</code>
|-
| GlowLamp brightness 100% at sunset || sunset || <code>light.turn_on</code>, brightness 255
|-
| GlowLamp brightness 50% at 11pm || 23:00 || <code>light.turn_on</code>, brightness 128
|-
| GlowLamp off at 12:30am || 00:30 || <code>light.turn_off</code>
|}

9am rather than sunset-minus-an-hour: the interior stays dark well into the
morning. 12:30am rather than midnight: a midnight OFF would blank everything at
the exact moment the New Year effect fires.

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml">
- id: glowlamp_on_at_0900
  alias: GlowLamp on at 9am
  triggers:
    - trigger: time
      at: "09:00:00"
  actions:
    - action: light.turn_on
      target:
        entity_id: [light.castor, light.pollux]
  mode: single

- id: glowlamp_off_at_0030
  alias: GlowLamp off at 12:30am
  triggers:
    - trigger: time
      at: "00:30:00"
  actions:
    - action: light.turn_off
      target:
        entity_id: [light.castor, light.pollux]
  mode: single
</syntaxhighlight>

Brightness is 0&ndash;255, so 255 is 100% and 128 is 50%. The lamp persists both
power and brightness, so the morning ON does not need to set a brightness: it
comes back at whatever the 11pm automation last left.

=== Brightness and power arrive together ===

The JSON light schema carries <code>state</code> and <code>brightness</code> in one
message, so '''<code>light.turn_on</code> with a brightness switches the lamp on''' whether
or not that was the intent. An unconditional brightness automation will undo
someone deliberately switching a lamp off.

The two brightness automations are therefore conditioned on the lamp already
being on:

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml">
  conditions:
    - condition: state
      entity_id: [light.castor, light.pollux]
      state: "on"
      match: any
</syntaxhighlight>

The GlowKitchen equivalents need no such condition, because
<code>SET_BRIGHTNESS</code> on those strips does not touch power. This is the one
place the two fleets genuinely differ.

=== Fitting the house-off routine ===

Because the lamps are real entities, house-wide automations pick them up for
free &mdash; an "everything off" routine that targets lights, an area or a group
reaches Castor and Pollux with nothing added.

The GlowKitchen strips cannot do this. They publish no discovery config, so they
are not entities, and every automation that touches them publishes to
<code>lights/all/cmd</code> by hand. Any new automation meant to cover the whole
workshop has to do both:

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml">
  actions:
    - action: light.turn_off
      target:
        entity_id: [light.castor, light.pollux]
    - action: mqtt.publish
      data:
        topic: lights/all/cmd
        payload: "OFF"
        retain: false
</syntaxhighlight>

'''Never retain a command.''' A retained command replays on every device reconnect;
stale retained brightness messages pinning the whole fleet is a bug the
GlowKitchen strips have already been through.

=== Adding a lamp ===

Set its broker and it appears. Then add its entity to each automation's
<code>entity_id</code> list &mdash; or make a light group and target the group, which
makes the third lamp free. The GlowKitchen <code>--all</code> equivalent does not
exist here, because there is no script in the path: Home Assistant is talking to
entities.

=== What MQTT does not cover ===

'''Firmware updates.''' <code>/api/ota/update</code> is REST only, so an overnight update
automation needs a <code>rest_command</code> or <code>shell_command</code> block in
<code>configuration.yaml</code> and the filesystem access that implies. Usually not
worth it: each lamp already checks daily at 15:00 on its own. Drive it from Home
Assistant only when the ''timing'' matters &mdash; picking the hour, and the same
hour for every lamp.

== Firmware updates ==

Lamps update themselves from GitHub releases. A lamp checks 15 seconds after
joining WiFi, once a day at 15:00 local, and whenever asked.

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
./scripts/find_devices.sh --update                     # every lamp with an update
curl -X POST http://castor-lamp.local/api/ota/update   # one lamp: check, install if newer
</syntaxhighlight>

<code>/api/ota/update</code> is the scheduler's endpoint: it does nothing at all on a
lamp that is current, so it is safe to fire at every lamp nightly.

A lamp stops answering for 10&ndash;30 seconds while it downloads, then reboots.

=== Cutting a release ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
# bump FIRMWARE_VERSION in src/version.h first, and commit
./scripts/release.sh 0.0.6
./scripts/find_devices.sh --update
</syntaxhighlight>

The script refuses to tag if the version does not match <code>src/version.h</code>, if the
working tree is dirty, or if the binary would overflow the 1,966,080-byte OTA
slot &mdash; a build that overflows uploads fine over USB and then fails silently
over the air.

'''Cut the release before flashing a new version by hand.''' Versions are compared by
tag equality, not ordering, so a lamp running firmware newer than the latest
release will quietly install the older one. That also makes re-tagging an older
release a supported way to roll back.

== Building ==

<syntaxhighlight lang="bash">
pio run                      # release, the build that ships
pio run -e esp32c3-debug     # verbose logging, USB only
pio run -t upload            # flash over USB
pio device monitor           # 115200
</syntaxhighlight>

Copy <code>lib/Config/Config.h.sample</code> to <code>lib/Config/Config.h</code> before the first
build. It is gitignored and holds the device name and the OTA repo; there are no
secrets in it.

== Troubleshooting ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Symptom !! Cause
|-
| Lamp not found by a scan || mDNS does not cross VLANs. An unprovisioned lamp is not on the network at all &mdash; look for its setup AP.
|-
| A scan lists lamps that do not exist || Stale mDNS records from a rename, cached by the browsing machine. They expire on their own.
|-
| <code>.local</code> name does not resolve || A cold mDNS cache can take longer to answer than a short HTTP timeout. The scripts resolve to an address first for this reason.
|-
| Entity never appears in Home Assistant || The lamp is not connected to the broker &mdash; check the MQTT line on its settings page.
|-
| Entity stuck "unavailable" || The lamp is down, or pointed at a different broker.
|-
| MQTT commands do nothing || <code>state</code> must be <code>"ON"</code>, not <code>true</code>.
|-
| Lamp reverts after every reconnect || A retained message on the command topic. Clear it with <code>mosquitto_pub -r -n -t glowlamp/<host>/set</code>.
|-
| Ring looks washed out or white || Colors too far apart on the wheel in ''flicker'', which mixes by design. Use colors that are close together.
|}

